---
import FAQ from '../FAQ.astro';
/* Answers were rewritten on 2026-09-21 when the client confirmed the outpatient
   continuum is open. They describe the level of care and how it connects to the
   rest of the continuum, and deliberately stop short of days per week, group
   times and program capacity, none of which has been confirmed. */
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Does Buena Vista have an intensive outpatient program?',
    a: "Yes. IOP runs at our Chandler facility as part of a full continuum of care, alongside medical detox, residential treatment, partial hospitalization and outpatient treatment. Most people reach it as a step down from residential care, but it is also a reasonable starting point for somebody who is already stable and has somewhere settled to live.",
  },
  {
    q: 'How is IOP different from residential treatment?',
    a: "Mainly where you sleep. In residential treatment you live here and the day is built around the program. In IOP you live at home, or in sober living, and come in for treatment for part of the day, several days a week. The clinical work is much the same — groups, individual therapy, relapse-prevention — at a dose that leaves room for the rest of your life.",
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does IOP last?',
    a: "It depends on where you are when you start and how things go once you do. The outpatient phase of our continuum typically runs 45 to 90 days, and longer where that is what somebody needs. Your case manager reviews it with you as you go rather than holding you to a number fixed on day one.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I keep working or studying while I attend?',
    a: "That is what this level of care is designed for. Schedules are built to flex around work, school and family responsibilities, and they grow with you as those things gradually come back in. Bring your actual week to the call and admissions will work the timing out with you.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Do you help with housing during outpatient treatment?',
    a: "Yes. Many people pair this phase with a sober living home for added structure, and sober living placement assistance is part of what our case managers do. We do not run the housing ourselves, so the help is in knowing the local landscape and lining it up alongside your program.",
  },
  {
    q: 'What if I am still drinking or using?',
    a: "Then medical detox is the safer first step, and it is in the same building. Outpatient care assumes your body is already stable. Call and describe what the last few days have looked like — admissions will tell you honestly which level of care fits, and nothing about that conversation commits you to anything.",
  },
];
---
<FAQ
  faqs={faqs}
  eyebrow="Straight answers"
  heading="Questions about IOP"
  bg="bg-cream"
/>
